  "preamble": "The voiceless velar stop [k] debuccalizes to the glottal fricative [h], losing its oral closure entirely. It is a regular sound law in several unrelated families: Hungarian h- before back vowels (hal 'fish' vs Finnish kala), Comorian among the Sabaki Bantu languages, and Malagasy, Toba Batak and Chamorro within Austronesian. Finnish shows it before coronal stops (tehdä < *tektäk), and Tuscan gorgia realizes post-vocalic /k/ as [h]. The Germanic h- of Grimm's law (Latin cornu vs English horn) went through an intermediate [x] and belongs to k > x and x > h.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Debuccalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Finnish",
      "languageFamily": "Finnic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Finnic *tektäk 'to do'",
          "to": "tehdä",
          "note": "Finnish *k became h before a coronal stop (*kt > ht); compare Estonian tegema."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Finnic *näktäk 'to see'",
          "to": "nähdä"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Finnic *läktedäk 'to leave'",
          "to": "lähteä"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Tuscan Italian",
      "languageFamily": "Italo-Dalmatian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "identificare /identifiˈkare/",
          "to": "[ˌidentifiˈhaːɾe]",
          "note": "Tuscan gorgia: post-vocalic /k/ is realized as [h]."
        },
        {
          "from": "la casa /la ˈkasa/ 'the house'",
          "to": "[la ˈhaːsa]",
          "note": "After a doubling word the stop stays: tre case [ˌtre kˈkaːse] 'three houses'."
        }
      ],
      "isAllophone": true
    },
    {
      "language": "Hungarian (before back vowels)",
